(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_chemistry_thepblockelements=[{id:"neet-2026r-q060",pn:60,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Halogens with conc. H\u2082SO\u2084 and MnO\u2082",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement-I : Heating NaCl with concentrated H\u2082SO\u2084 and MnO\u2082 results in oxidation of Mn.
Statement-II : Heating NaI with concentrated H\u2082SO\u2084 and MnO\u2082 results in reduction of Mn.
In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are incorrect","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct"],c:2,s:`Statement-I is false.
2NaCl + MnO\u2082 + 2H\u2082SO\u2084 \u2192 Na\u2082SO\u2084 + MnSO\u2084 + 2H\u2082O + Cl\u2082 (Mn goes from +4 in MnO\u2082 to +2 in MnSO\u2084).
So Mn is reduced, not oxidised.
Statement-II is true.
2NaI + MnO\u2082 + 2H\u2082SO\u2084 \u2192 Na\u2082SO\u2084 + MnSO\u2084 + I\u2082 + 2H\u2082O (Mn goes from +4 to +2).
So Mn is reduced.`},{id:"neet-2026-q062",pn:62,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Group trends and catenation",q:"Identify the incorrect statement from the following:",o:["Carbon has the ability to form p\u03C0-p\u03C0 multiple bond with itself.","ECl\u2083 (E = B and Al) is a monomer when E = B and a dimer when E = Al.","Oxygen exhibits only \u20132 oxidation state.","The order of catenation property of Group 14 elements is C >> Si > Ge \u2248 Sn."],c:2,s:`(1) Carbon forms p\u03C0-p\u03C0 multiple bonds with itself, as in C=C and C\u2261C. Correct.
(2) BCl\u2083 does not dimerise, but AlCl\u2083 forms the dimer Al\u2082Cl\u2086. Correct.
(3) Oxygen shows oxidation state 0 in O\u2082, \u20132 in oxides and \u20131 in peroxides. So 'only \u20132' is incorrect.
(4) Catenation in group 14 follows C >> Si > Ge \u2248 Sn. Correct.`},{id:"neet-2026-q067",pn:67,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Group 15 bonding and catenation",q:"Identify the incorrect statement from the following:",o:["P(C\u2082H\u2085)\u2083 and As(C\u2086H\u2085)\u2083 form d\u03C0-d\u03C0 bond with transition metals.","Nitrogen can form d\u03C0-p\u03C0 bond with oxygen.","Nitrogen can form p\u03C0-p\u03C0 multiple bonds with itself.","Phosphorus, arsenic and antimony show catenation property."],c:1,s:`Nitrogen and oxygen have no d-orbitals, so they cannot form a d\u03C0-p\u03C0 bond. Statement (2) is incorrect.
Phosphorus and arsenic have vacant d-orbitals, so they can form d\u03C0-d\u03C0 bonds with transition metals.
Phosphorus, arsenic and antimony show catenation.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q090",pn:90,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Potash alum formula",q:"In potash alum, the ratio of K\u207A and SO\u2084\xB2\u207B ions is",o:["2 : 1","2 : 3","3 : 2","1 : 2"],c:3,s:`Potash alum is K\u2082SO\u2084\xB7Al\u2082(SO\u2084)\u2083\xB724H\u2082O.
No. of K\u207A = 2.
No. of SO\u2084\xB2\u207B = 4.
Ratio K\u207A : SO\u2084\xB2\u207B = 2/4 = 1/2.`},{id:"neet-2025-q052",pn:52,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Group 15 hydrides and oxides",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : Like nitrogen that can form ammonia, arsenic can form arsine.
Statement II : Antimony cannot form antimony pentoxide.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],c:2,s:`All group 15 elements form hydrides of EH\u2083 type.
Nitrogen forms ammonia (NH\u2083) and arsenic forms arsine (AsH\u2083).
All group 15 elements form two types of oxides: E\u2082O\u2083 and E\u2082O\u2085.
Antimony forms antimony pentoxide Sb\u2082O\u2085.
So Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect.`},{id:"neet-2024-q065",pn:65,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Hydrides of group 16 and hydrogen bonding",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement-I : The boiling point of hydrides of Group 16 elements follow the order H\u2082O > H\u2082Te > H\u2082Se > H\u2082S.
Statement-II : On the basis of molecular mass, H\u2082O is expected to have lower boiling point than the other members of the group but due to the presence of extensive H-bonding in H\u2082O, it has higher boiling point.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["Statement I true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false."],c:2,s:`Statement-I is correct: boiling points follow H\u2082O > H\u2082Te > H\u2082Se > H\u2082S.
Statement-II is correct: H\u2082O has strong intermolecular H-bonding, so its boiling point is higher than that of the other hydrides.
The order from H\u2082S to H\u2082Te rises because the molar mass increases.
Both statements are true.`},{id:"neet-2024-q071",pn:71,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Oxidation states of group 16",q:"Among Group 16 elements, which one does NOT show \u20132 oxidation state ?",o:["Te","Po","O","Se"],c:1,s:`Oxygen shows \u20132, \u20131, +1 and +2.
Selenium shows \u20132, +2, +4 and +6.
Tellurium shows \u20132, +2, +4 and +6.
Polonium shows only +2 and +4, so it does not show \u20132.`},{id:"neet-2023-q068",pn:68,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Allotropes of carbon",q:`Match List-I with List-II. :
List-I: A. Coke  B. Diamond  C. Fullerene  D. Graphite
List-II: I. Carbon atoms are sp\xB3 hybridised.  II. Used as a dry lubricant  III. Used as reducing agent  IV. Cage like molecules
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II"],c:3,s:`Coke is widely used as a reducing agent in metallurgy: A-III.
In diamond every carbon is sp\xB3 hybridised and bonded tetrahedrally to four others: B-I.
Fullerene has five- and six-membered rings forming a cage: C-IV.
Graphite is soft and slippery, so it is a dry lubricant even at high temperature: D-II.`},{id:"neet-2023-q080",pn:80,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Inert pair effect",q:"Taking stability as the factor, which one of the following represents correct relationship ?",o:["TI > TI\u2083","TCl\u2083 > TCl","InI\u2083 > InI","AlCl > AlCl\u2083"],c:0,s:`Down a group, poor shielding by inner d and f orbitals raises the effective nuclear charge.
The ns electrons are held tightly and do not take part in bonding.
So the +1 state becomes more stable for heavier elements.
E\xB0 for In\xB3\u207A|In\u207A = \u20130.16 V and for Tl\xB3\u207A|Tl\u207A = +1.6 V.
Hence TlI is more stable than TlI\u2083.`},{id:"neet-2023-q082",pn:82,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Noble gases uses",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R :
Assertion A : Helium is used to dilute oxygen in diving apparatus.
Reason R : Helium has high solubility in O\u2082.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true and R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false."],c:2,s:`Helium is used to dilute oxygen in diving apparatus because it has very low solubility in blood.
Gases mix easily with each other.
Note: printed answer 3; my working gives 4 because helium is used in diving apparatus for its very low solubility in blood, so R (high solubility) is false.`},{id:"neet-2023-q096",pn:96,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Oxoacids of sulphur",q:`Match List-I with List-II :
List-I (Oxoacids of Sulphur): A. Peroxodisulphuric acid, B. Sulphuric acid, C. Pyrosulphuric acid, D. Sulphurous acid
List-II (Bonds): I. Two S\u2013OH, Four S=O, One S\u2013O\u2013S; II. Two S\u2013OH, One S=O; III. Two S\u2013OH, Four S=O, One S\u2013O\u2013O\u2013S; IV. Two S\u2013OH, Two S=O
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["A\u2013III, B\u2013IV, C\u2013II, D\u2013I","A\u2013I, B\u2013III, C\u2013II, D\u2013IV","A\u2013III, B\u2013IV, C\u2013I, D\u2013II","A\u2013I, B\u2013III, C\u2013IV, D\u2013II"],c:2,s:`H\u2082S\u2082O\u2088 has an S\u2013O\u2013O\u2013S link with two S\u2013OH and four S=O: A matches III.
H\u2082SO\u2084 has two S\u2013OH and two S=O: B matches IV.
H\u2082S\u2082O\u2087 has an S\u2013O\u2013S link with two S\u2013OH and four S=O: C matches I.
H\u2082SO\u2083 has two S\u2013OH and one S=O: D matches II.`,sfig:{w:464,h:400,alt:"Structures of peroxodisulphuric acid H2S2O8, sulphuric acid H2SO4, pyrosulphuric acid H2S2O7 and sulphurous acid H2SO3 with their S=O, S-OH, S-O-S and S-O-O-S bonds.",d:[["t",56,60,"S","m"],["t",193.6,60,"S","m"],["t",56,16,"O","m"],["l",58.4,45.6,58.4,22.4,""],["l",53.6,45.6,53.6,22.4,""],["t",193.6,16,"O","m"],["l",196,45.6,196,22.4,""],["l",191.2,45.6,191.2,22.4,""],["t",16,97.6,"O","m"],["l",46.8,61.4,21.9,84.7,""],["l",50.1,64.9,25.2,88.2,""],["t",61.6,109.6,"OH","m"],["l",57.2,66.3,60.3,93.7,""],["t",96,93.6,"O","m"],["l",64,62.7,88.6,83.4,""],["t",148,93.6,"O","m"],["l",105.6,89.6,138.4,89.6,""],["l",185.2,62.2,155.7,83.9,""],["t",196,109.6,"OH","m"],["l",194.1,66.4,195.4,93.6,""],["t",233.6,96,"O","m"],["l",199.7,64.7,224.3,86.8,""],["l",202.9,61.2,227.5,83.3,""],["t",121.6,144,"Peroxodisulphuric acid","m"],["t",132,168,"(H\u2082S\u2082O\u2088)","m"],["t",393.6,60,"S","m"],["t",393.6,16,"O","m"],["l",396,45.6,396,22.4,""],["l",391.2,45.6,391.2,22.4,""],["t",340,100,"HO","m"],["l",385.3,62.2,350.3,88.3,""],["t",397.6,111.2,"OH","m"],["l",394.4,66.4,396.7,95.2,""],["t",433.6,98.4,"O","m"],["l",399.4,64.9,424.4,88.9,""],["l",402.8,61.5,427.8,85.5,""],["t",388,138.4,"Sulphuric acid","m"],["t",400,160.8,"(H\u2082SO\u2084)","m"],["t",56,277.6,"S","m"],["t",144,277.6,"S","m"],["t",56,233.6,"O","m"],["l",58.4,263.2,58.4,240,""],["l",53.6,263.2,53.6,240,""],["t",144,233.6,"O","m"],["l",146.4,263.2,146.4,240,""],["l",141.6,263.2,141.6,240,""],["t",16,316,"O","m"],["l",46.8,279.1,21.8,303.1,""],["l",50.2,282.5,25.2,306.5,""],["t",61.6,327.2,"OH","m"],["l",57.2,283.9,60.3,311.3,""],["t",96,314.4,"O","m"],["l",63.7,280.6,88.9,303.9,""],["l",103.6,304.6,135.7,279.9,""],["t",148.8,327.2,"OH","m"],["l",145,284,147.6,311.3,""],["t",184,316,"O","m"],["l",149.8,282.5,174.8,306.5,""],["l",153.2,279.1,178.2,303.1,""],["t",100,357.6,"Pyrosulphuric acid","m"],["t",124,381.6,"(H\u2082S\u2082O\u2087)","m"],["t",388,277.6,"S","m"],["p","M388 260.8C378.4 251.2 368 244.8 369.6 235.2C372 222.4 404 222.4 406.4 235.2C408 244.8 397.6 251.2 388 260.8Z",""],["c",385,236.8,1.2,"F"],["c",391,236.8,1.2,"F"],["t",366.4,316,"HO","m"],["l",382.9,282.7,372.7,300.8,""],["t",392,319.2,"HO","m"],["l",389,284,390.8,302.5,""],["t",409.6,316,"O","m"],["l",391,283.8,402.4,304.1,""],["l",395.2,281.5,406.6,301.8,""],["t",388,354.4,"Sulphurous acid","m"],["t",396,378.4,"(H\u2082SO\u2083)","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2022-q054",pn:54,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Structure of diborane",q:"Which of the following statement is not correct about diborane?",o:["The four terminal B-H bonds are two centre two electron bonds.","The four terminal Hydrogen atoms and the two Boron atoms lie in one plane.","Both the Boron atoms are sp\xB2 hybridised.","There are two 3-centre-2-electron bonds."],c:2,s:`Each boron atom in diborane uses sp\xB3 hybrid orbitals, not sp\xB2.
So the statement that both borons are sp\xB2 is wrong.`,sfig:{w:260,h:150,alt:"Diborane: two boron atoms each with two terminal hydrogens, two bridging hydrogens above and below joined to both borons; arrows from each boron labelled sp3 hybridised",d:[["c",80,62,7,""],["t",80,65.5,"B","ms"],["c",180,62,7,""],["t",180,65.5,"B","ms"],["t",42,42,"H","m"],["t",42,90,"H","m"],["t",218,42,"H","m"],["t",218,90,"H","m"],["l",75,57,50,42,""],["l",75,67,50,82,""],["l",185,57,210,42,""],["l",185,67,210,82,""],["p","M78 55C82 30 100 20 120 20",""],["p","M140 20C160 20 178 30 182 55",""],["t",130,26,"H","m"],["p","M86 58C112 40 148 40 174 58",""],["p","M86 66C112 84 148 84 174 66",""],["p","M78 69C82 94 100 104 120 104",""],["p","M140 104C160 104 178 94 182 69",""],["t",130,106,"H","m"],["p","M112 124C88 126 72 108 76 78","a"],["p","M148 124C172 126 188 108 184 78","a"],["t",130,128,"sp\xB3","m"],["t",130,144,"hybridised","ms"]]}},{id:"neet-2022r-q055",pn:55,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Compounds of boron",q:`Match List - I with List - II :
List-I (Compounds): (a) Borax (b) Kernite (c) Orthoboric acid (d) Borax Bead
List-II (Molecular formula): (i) NaBO\u2082 (ii) Na\u2082B\u2084O\u2087\xB74H\u2082O (iii) H\u2083BO\u2083 (iv) Na\u2082B\u2084O\u2087\xB710H\u2082O
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["(a) - (i), (b) - (iii), (c) - (iv), (d) - (ii)","(a) - (iv), (b) - (ii), (c) - (iii), (d) - (i)","(a) - (ii), (b) - (iv), (c) - (iii), (d) - (i)","(a) - (iii), (b) - (i), (c) - (iv), (d) - (ii)"],c:1,s:`Borax is Na\u2082B\u2084O\u2087\xB710H\u2082O.
Kernite is Na\u2082B\u2084O\u2087\xB74H\u2082O.
Orthoboric acid is H\u2083BO\u2083.
Borax bead is NaBO\u2082.`},{id:"neet-2022-q057",pn:57,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Boiling points of group 16 hydrides",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : The boiling points of the following hydrides of group 16 elements increases in the order \u2013 H\u2082O < H\u2082S < H\u2082Se < H\u2082Te
Statement II : The boiling points of these hydrides increase with increase in molar mass.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:0,s:`Boiling points (K): H\u2082O 373, H\u2082S 213, H\u2082Se 232, H\u2082Te 269.
H\u2082O has the highest value because of intermolecular hydrogen bonding.
So the order in Statement I is wrong, and the boiling points do not simply rise with molar mass.
Both statements are incorrect.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q060",pn:60,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Ostwald process for nitric acid",q:"Which of the following reactions is a part of the large scale industrial preparation of nitric acid",o:["Cu(NO\u2083)\u2082 + 2NO\u2082 + 2H\u2082O \u2192 4HNO\u2083 + Cu (Pt, 500 K, 9 bar)","NaNO\u2083 + H\u2082SO\u2084 \u2192 NaHSO\u2084 + HNO\u2083 (Pt, 500 K, 9 bar)","4NH\u2083 + 5O\u2082 (from air) \u2192 4NO + 6H\u2082O (Pt, 500 K, 9 bar)","4HPO\u2083 + 2N\u2082O\u2085 \u2192 4HNO\u2083 + P\u2084O\u2081\u2080 (Pt, 500 K, 9 bar)"],c:2,s:`Nitric acid is made on a large scale by Ostwald's process.
Step 1: 4NH\u2083 + 5O\u2082 \u2192 4NO + 6H\u2082O, with Pt/Rh gauze catalyst at 500 K and 9 bar.
Step 2: 2NO + O\u2082 \u2192 2NO\u2082.
Step 3: 3NO\u2082 + H\u2082O \u2192 2HNO\u2083 + NO.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q063",pn:63,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Borax on heating",q:"Na\u2082B\u2084O\u2087 \u2192(heat) X + NaBO\u2082 in the above reaction the product \u201CX\u201D is :",o:["NaB\u2083O\u2085","H\u2083BO\u2083","B\u2082O\u2083","Na\u2082B\u2082O\u2085"],c:2,s:`Strong heating of borax gives boric anhydride and sodium metaborate.
Na\u2082B\u2084O\u2087 \u2192 B\u2082O\u2083 + 2NaBO\u2082.
So X is B\u2082O\u2083.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q076",pn:76,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Oxidising power of halogens",q:`Flourine is a stronger oxidising agent than chlorine because:
(a) F-F bond has a low enthalpy of dissociation.
(b) Flouride ion (F\u207B) has high hydration enthalpy.
(c) Electron gain enthalpy of flourine is less negative than chlorine.
(d) Flourine has a very small size.
Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given:`,o:["(b) and (c) only","(a) and (b) only","(a) and (c) only","(a) and (d) only"],c:1,s:`Oxidising power depends on the total energy released when X\u2082 becomes X\u207B(aq).
This has three steps: dissociation of X\u2082 (endothermic), electron gain (exothermic) and hydration of X\u207B (exothermic).
Fluorine has a low F-F bond dissociation enthalpy, so less energy is spent breaking the bond.
F\u207B has a very high hydration enthalpy, so a lot of energy is released.
So more energy is released overall for fluorine, and statements (a) and (b) are the reasons.`},{id:"neet-2022-q079",pn:79,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Allotropes of carbon",q:"Choose the correct statement:",o:["Diamond is covalent and graphite is ionic.","Diamond is sp\xB3 hybridised and graphite is sp hybridized.","Both diamond and graphite are used as dry lubricants.","Diamond and graphite have two dimensional network."],c:1,s:`Diamond: sp\xB3 carbon, covalent solid, 3-D network, not a lubricant.
Graphite: sp\xB2 carbon, covalent solid, layered structure, used as a dry lubricant.
The printed key is (2).
Note: printed answer 2; my working gives that graphite is sp\xB2 (not sp), so option 2 is also wrong as written; the key is kept as printed.`},{id:"neet-2022-q081",pn:81,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Interhalogen compounds",q:`Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A): ICl is more reactive than I\u2082.
Reason (R): I\u2013Cl bond is weaker than I\u2013I bond.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)."],c:3,s:`Interhalogens are generally more reactive than halogens (except fluorine).
The reason is that the X\u2013X' bond is weaker than the X\u2013X bond.
So I\u2013Cl, being weaker than I\u2013I, makes ICl more reactive. (R) explains (A).`},{id:"neet-2022r-q096",pn:96,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Calcination",q:"Which one of the following is not a calcination reaction?",o:["CaCO\u2083 + 2HCl \u2192 CaCl\u2082 + H\u2082O + CO\u2082 (\u0394)","ZnCO\u2083 \u2192 ZnO + CO\u2082 (\u0394)","Fe\u2082O\u2083\xB7xH\u2082O \u2192 Fe\u2082O\u2083 + xH\u2082O (\u0394)","CaCO\u2083\xB7MgCO\u2083 \u2192 CaO + MgO + 2CO\u2082 (\u0394)"],c:0,s:`Calcination means heating an ore in little or no air so that volatile matter escapes and the metal oxide is left.
ZnCO\u2083 \u2192 ZnO + CO\u2082 is calcination.
Fe\u2082O\u2083\xB7xH\u2082O \u2192 Fe\u2082O\u2083 + xH\u2082O is calcination.
CaCO\u2083\xB7MgCO\u2083 \u2192 CaO + MgO + 2CO\u2082 is calcination.
CaCO\u2083 reacting with HCl is just an acid reaction, not thermal decomposition, so it is not calcination.
Note: Chapter 'General principles of isolation of elements' is not in the chapter list; nearest chapter used.`},{id:"neet-2021-q064",pn:64,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Acidity of hydrogen halides",q:`Statement I : Acid strength increases in the order given as HF << HCl << HBr << HI.
Statement II : As the size of the elements F, Cl, Br, I increases down the group, the bond strength of HF, HCl, HBr and HI decreases and so the acid strength increases.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true","Both statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is correct but statement II is false"],c:1,s:`Going down group 17 the halogen atom gets bigger, so the H\u2013X bond gets longer and weaker.
A weaker bond lets H\u207A leave more easily, so acid strength rises: HF < HCl < HBr < HI.
Statement I is true and Statement II gives the right reason, so both are true.`},{id:"neet-2021-q071",pn:71,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Blast furnace",q:"The maximum temperature that can be achieved in blast furnace is :",o:["Upto 5000 K","Upto 1200 K","Upto 2200 K","Upto 1900 K"],c:2,s:`In the iron blast furnace the hottest zone, near the tuyeres at the bottom, reaches about 2200 K (NCERT figure shows 2170 K).
So the answer is up to 2200 K.`},{id:"neet-2021-q078",pn:78,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Purification of metals",q:"Which one of the following methods can be used to obtain highly pure metal which is liquid at room temperature?",o:["Zone refining","Electrolysis","Chromatography","Distillation"],c:3,s:`Distillation purifies metals with low boiling points, like Hg (liquid at room temperature) and Zn.
The impurities are left behind as the metal vaporises and condenses again.`},{id:"neet-2021-q083",pn:83,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Noble gases",q:"Noble gases are named because of their inertness towards reactivity. Identify an incorrect statement about them.",o:["Noble gases have large positive values of electron gain enthalpy","Noble gases are sparingly soluble in water","Noble gases have very high melting and boiling points","Noble gases have weak dispersion forces"],c:2,s:`Noble gases are monatomic and held only by weak dispersion forces.
So their melting and boiling points are very low, not very high.
Statement 3 is therefore incorrect. The other three statements are true.`},{id:"neet-2021-q089",pn:89,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Trends in group properties",q:"In which one of the following arrangements the given sequence is not strictly according to the properties indicated against it?",o:["CO\u2082 < SiO\u2082 < SnO\u2082 < PbO\u2082 : Increasing oxiding power","HF < HCl < HBr < HI : Increasing acidic strength","H\u2082O < H\u2082S < H\u2082Se < H\u2082Te : Increasing pKa values","NH\u2083 < PH\u2083 < AsH\u2083 < SbH\u2083 : Increasing acidic character"],c:2,s:`Going down group 16, the E\u2013H bond weakens, so the hydrides become stronger acids.
A stronger acid has a lower pKa, so the pKa order is H\u2082O > H\u2082S > H\u2082Se > H\u2082Te (decreasing).
So sequence (3), which says increasing pKa, is wrong. The other three are correct.`},{id:"neet-2020-q156",pn:156,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Oxoacids of sulphur",q:"Which of the following oxoacid of sulphur has \u2013 O \u2013 O \u2013 linkage ?",o:["H\u2082SO\u2084, sulphuric acid","H\u2082S\u2082O\u2088, peroxodisulphuric acid","H\u2082S\u2082O\u2087, pyrosulphuric acid","H\u2082SO\u2083, sulphurous acid"],c:1,s:`Peroxodisulphuric acid is HO\u2013SO\u2082\u2013O\u2013O\u2013SO\u2082\u2013OH.
It has a peroxy (\u2013O\u2013O\u2013) link between two sulphur atoms.
The other acids have no O\u2013O bond.
Note: Option 4 is printed as H\u2082SP\u2083 in the paper; written here as H\u2082SO\u2083 (sulphurous acid).`,sfig:{w:245,h:118,alt:"H2S2O8: HO-SO2-O-O-SO2-OH with the O-O group boxed and labelled peroxy linkage",d:[["r",89,30,68,22],["t",8,45,"H","m"],["t",42,45,"O","m"],["t",73,45,"S","m"],["t",105,45,"O","m"],["t",138,45,"O","m"],["t",169,45,"S","m"],["t",200,45,"O","m"],["t",232,45,"H","m"],["l",15,41,35,41],["l",49,41,66,41],["l",80,41,98,41],["l",113,41,130,41],["l",146,41,162,41],["l",176,41,192,41],["l",208,41,224,41],["l",71,21,71,34],["l",75,21,75,34],["t",73,16,"O","m"],["l",71,49,71,62],["l",75,49,75,62],["t",73,76,"O","m"],["l",167,21,167,34],["l",171,21,171,34],["t",169,16,"O","m"],["l",167,49,167,62],["l",171,49,171,62],["t",169,76,"O","m"],["l",122,92,122,56,"a"],["t",122,107,"Peroxy linkage","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2020-q158",pn:158,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Carbon and its compounds",q:`Identify the correct statements from the following :
(a) CO\u2082(g) is used as refrigerant for ice-cream and frozen food.
(b) The structure of C\u2086\u2080 contains twelve six carbon rings and twenty five carbon rings.
(c) ZSM-5, a type of zeolite, is used to convert alcohols into gasoline
(d) CO is colourless and odourless gas`,o:["(a) and (c) only","(b) and (c) only","(c) and (d) only","(a), (b) and (c) only"],c:2,s:`(a) Solid CO\u2082 (dry ice) is the refrigerant, not the gas, so (a) is wrong.
(b) C\u2086\u2080 has twenty six-membered and twelve five-membered rings, so (b) is wrong.
(c) ZSM-5 converts alcohols to gasoline - correct.
(d) CO is a colourless, odourless gas - correct.`},{id:"neet-2019-q139",pn:139,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Group 14",q:"Which of the following is incorrect statement ?",o:["GeX\u2084(X = F, Cl, Br, I) is more stable than GeX\u2082","SnF\u2084 is ionic in nature","PbF\u2084 is covalent in nature","SiCl\u2084 is easily hydrolysed"],c:2,s:`PbF\u2084 is an ionic compound, since fluoride is small and strongly electronegative.
So the statement that it is covalent is incorrect.`},{id:"neet-2019-q143",pn:143,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Oxides and structures",q:"The correct structure of tribromooctaoxide is -",o:["(1)","(2)","(3)","(4)"],c:2,s:`The formula is Br\u2083O\u2088 with three bromine atoms joined in a chain Br\u2013Br\u2013Br.
The structure with no charges has 8 oxygens, all double bonded to bromine: three on the left Br, two on the middle Br, three on the right Br.
This is option 3.
Note: Drawings (1), (2) and (4) are described only roughly; they all carry negative charges on some O, option 3 has none.`,fig:{w:470,h:182,alt:"Four drawings of a Br\u2013Br\u2013Br chain with eight oxygen atoms attached, some as =O and some as O\u207B; option 3 has all eight as =O with no charges.",d:[["t",0,50,"(1)",""],["t",62,50,"Br","m"],["t",98,50,"Br","m"],["t",134,50,"Br","m"],["l",72,46,88,46,""],["l",108,46,124,46,""],["l",52,46,40,46,""],["t",34,50,"O","m"],["t",22,42,"\u207B",""],["l",56.8,39.6,50.1,26.2,""],["l",60,38,53.3,24.6,""],["t",49,24,"O","m"],["l",58.4,53.2,51.7,66.6,""],["t",49,76,"O","m"],["t",37,68,"\u207B",""],["l",96.2,38,96.2,26,""],["l",99.8,38,99.8,26,""],["t",98,24,"O","m"],["l",99.8,54,99.8,66,""],["l",96.2,54,96.2,66,""],["t",98,76,"O","m"],["l",144,44.2,156,44.2,""],["l",144,47.8,156,47.8,""],["t",162,50,"O","m"],["l",137.6,38.8,144.3,25.4,""],["t",147,24,"O","m"],["t",152,16,"\u207B",""],["l",137.6,53.2,144.3,66.6,""],["t",147,76,"O","m"],["t",152,68,"\u207B",""],["t",260,50,"(2)",""],["t",322,50,"Br","m"],["t",358,50,"Br","m"],["t",394,50,"Br","m"],["l",332,46,348,46,""],["l",368,46,384,46,""],["l",312,47.8,300,47.8,""],["l",312,44.2,300,44.2,""],["t",294,50,"O","m"],["l",316.8,39.6,310.1,26.2,""],["l",320,38,313.3,24.6,""],["t",309,24,"O","m"],["l",320,54,313.3,67.4,""],["l",316.8,52.4,310.1,65.8,""],["t",309,76,"O","m"],["l",358,38,358,26,""],["t",358,24,"O","m"],["t",363,16,"\u207B",""],["l",358,54,358,66,""],["t",358,76,"O","m"],["t",363,68,"\u207B",""],["l",404,44.2,416,44.2,""],["l",404,47.8,416,47.8,""],["t",422,50,"O","m"],["t",427,42,"\u207B",""],["l",396,38,402.7,24.6,""],["l",399.2,39.6,405.9,26.2,""],["t",407,24,"O","m"],["l",399.2,52.4,405.9,65.8,""],["l",396,54,402.7,67.4,""],["t",407,76,"O","m"],["t",0,142,"(3)",""],["t",62,142,"Br","m"],["t",98,142,"Br","m"],["t",134,142,"Br","m"],["l",72,138,88,138,""],["l",108,138,124,138,""],["l",52,139.8,40,139.8,""],["l",52,136.2,40,136.2,""],["t",34,142,"O","m"],["l",56.8,131.6,50.1,118.2,""],["l",60,130,53.3,116.6,""],["t",49,116,"O","m"],["l",60,146,53.3,159.4,""],["l",56.8,144.4,50.1,157.8,""],["t",49,168,"O","m"],["l",96.2,130,96.2,118,""],["l",99.8,130,99.8,118,""],["t",98,116,"O","m"],["l",99.8,146,99.8,158,""],["l",96.2,146,96.2,158,""],["t",98,168,"O","m"],["l",144,136.2,156,136.2,""],["l",144,139.8,156,139.8,""],["t",162,142,"O","m"],["l",136,130,142.7,116.6,""],["l",139.2,131.6,145.9,118.2,""],["t",147,116,"O","m"],["l",139.2,144.4,145.9,157.8,""],["l",136,146,142.7,159.4,""],["t",147,168,"O","m"],["t",260,142,"(4)",""],["t",322,142,"Br","m"],["t",358,142,"Br","m"],["t",394,142,"Br","m"],["l",332,138,348,138,""],["l",368,138,384,138,""],["l",312,139.8,300,139.8,""],["l",312,136.2,300,136.2,""],["t",294,142,"O","m"],["l",316.8,131.6,310.1,118.2,""],["l",320,130,313.3,116.6,""],["t",309,116,"O","m"],["l",320,146,313.3,159.4,""],["l",316.8,144.4,310.1,157.8,""],["t",309,168,"O","m"],["l",356.2,130,356.2,118,""],["l",359.8,130,359.8,118,""],["t",358,116,"O","m"],["l",358,146,358,158,""],["t",358,168,"O","m"],["t",363,160,"\u207B",""],["l",404,136.2,416,136.2,""],["l",404,139.8,416,139.8,""],["t",422,142,"O","m"],["t",427,134,"\u207B",""],["l",396,130,402.7,116.6,""],["l",399.2,131.6,405.9,118.2,""],["t",407,116,"O","m"],["l",397.6,145.2,404.3,158.6,""],["t",407,168,"O","m"],["t",412,160,"\u207B",""]]}},{id:"neet-2019-q146",pn:146,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Industrial processes",q:`Match the following :
(a) Pure nitrogen (i) Chlorine
(b) Haber process (ii) Sulphuric acid
(c) Contact process (iii) Ammonia
(d) Deacon\u2019s process (iv) Sodium azide or Barium azide`,o:["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],c:1,s:`Pure N\u2082 is made by heating sodium or barium azide: Ba(N\u2083)\u2082 \u2192 Ba + 3N\u2082.
Haber process makes ammonia: N\u2082 + 3H\u2082 \u21CC 2NH\u2083.
Contact process makes sulphuric acid: 2SO\u2082 + O\u2082 \u21CC 2SO\u2083.
Deacon's process makes chlorine: 4HCl + O\u2082 \u2192 2H\u2082O + 2Cl\u2082 (CuCl\u2082 catalyst).
So (a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i).`},{id:"neet-2019-q149",pn:149,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Structure of PCl\u2085",q:"Identify the incorrect statement related to PCl\u2085 from the following -",o:["Axial P\u2013Cl bonds are longer than equatorial P\u2013Cl bonds","PCl\u2085 molecule is non-reactive","Three equatorial P\u2013Cl bonds make an angle of 120\xBA with each other","Two axial P\u2013Cl bonds make an angle of 180\xBA with each other"],c:1,s:`PCl\u2085 is trigonal bipyramidal: three equatorial P\u2013Cl bonds at 120\xBA and two axial bonds at 180\xBA.
Axial bonds are longer and weaker, so PCl\u2085 is quite reactive.
So the statement 'non-reactive' is incorrect.`,sfig:{w:130,h:112,alt:"PCl\u2085 with P at the centre: one Cl straight up and one straight down (axial) and three Cl in the equatorial plane.",d:[["t",60,60,"P","m"],["l",60,48,60,22,""],["t",60,18,"Cl","m"],["l",60,64,60,90,""],["t",60,102,"Cl","m"],["l",52.8,52.4,32,42,""],["t",24,42,"Cl","m"],["l",52.8,59.6,32,70,""],["t",24,78,"Cl","m"],["l",68,56,91,56,""],["t",100,60,"Cl","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2019-q161",pn:161,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Group 16 hydrides",q:"Which is the correct thermal stability order for H\u2082E (E = O, S, Se, Te and Po)?",o:["H\u2082Po < H\u2082Te < H\u2082Se < H\u2082S < H\u2082O","H\u2082Se < H\u2082Te < H\u2082Po < H\u2082O < H\u2082S","H\u2082S < H\u2082O < H\u2082Se < H\u2082Te < H\u2082Po","H\u2082O < H\u2082S < H\u2082Se < H\u2082Te < H\u2082Po"],c:0,s:`Going down the group the E\u2013H bond gets longer and weaker.
So thermal stability falls from H\u2082O to H\u2082Po.
Order: H\u2082Po < H\u2082Te < H\u2082Se < H\u2082S < H\u2082O.`},{id:"neet-2019-q167",pn:167,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Group 14 complexes",q:"Which of the following species is not stable?",o:["[Sn(OH)\u2086]\xB2\u207B","[SiCl\u2086]\xB2\u207B","[SiF\u2086]\xB2\u207B","[GeCl\u2086]\xB2\u207B"],c:1,s:`Silicon can expand its octahedron using d orbitals.
But six large Cl atoms cannot fit around the small Si atom, which gives steric crowding.
F is small, so [SiF\u2086]\xB2\u207B is stable, and Ge and Sn are large enough for their complexes.
So [SiCl\u2086]\xB2\u207B is not stable.`},{id:"neet-2019-q168",pn:168,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Noble gas compounds",q:`Match the Xenon compounds in Column-I with its structure in column-II and assign the correct code :
Column-I: (a) XeF\u2084 (b) XeF\u2086 (c) XeOF\u2084 (d) XeO\u2083
Column-II: (i) pyramidal (ii) square planar (iii) distorted octahedral (iv) square pyramidal`,o:["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)"],c:3,s:`XeF\u2084 has 4 bond pairs and 2 lone pairs: square planar.
XeF\u2086 has 6 bond pairs and 1 lone pair: distorted octahedral.
XeOF\u2084 has 5 bond pairs and 1 lone pair: square pyramidal.
XeO\u2083 has 3 bond pairs and 1 lone pair: pyramidal.
So (a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i).`,sfig:{w:440,h:450,alt:"Shapes of xenon compounds: XeF\u2084 square planar, XeF\u2086 distorted octahedral, XeOF\u2084 square pyramidal and XeO\u2083 pyramidal, each with its lone pair of electrons marked by dots on Xe.",d:[["t",13,61,"XeF\u2084",""],["t",124,56,"Xe","m"],["c",120,40,1,"F"],["c",128,40,1,"F"],["c",120,68,1,"F"],["c",128,68,1,"F"],["l",111.5,45.6,81,30.1,""],["t",73,30,"F","m"],["l",135.5,44,163.6,24.2,""],["t",171,23,"F","m"],["l",112.1,59.4,80.6,79.2,""],["t",73,88,"F","m"],["l",135.6,59.9,163.6,78.9,""],["t",171,88,"F","m"],["t",195,60,"Square planar",""],["t",13,219,"XeF\u2086",""],["t",124,192,"Xe","m"],["c",120,176,1,"F"],["c",128,176,1,"F"],["c",138,185,1,"F"],["c",138,193,1,"F"],["t",125,127,"F","m"],["t",73,160,"F","m"],["t",171,160,"F","m"],["t",73,224,"F","m"],["t",171,224,"F","m"],["t",125,250,"F","m"],["l",125,131,125,153,""],["l",90,153,166,153,""],["l",90,223,166,223,""],["l",125,223,125,238,""],["l",74,172,74,205,""],["l",172,172,172,205,""],["l",112.4,180.2,81,159,""],["l",135.6,180.2,167,159,""],["l",112.4,195.8,81,217,""],["l",135.6,195.8,167,217,""],["t",194,191,"Distorted octahedral",""],["t",13,331,"XeOF\u2084",""],["t",124,330,"Xe","m"],["c",120,340,1,"F"],["c",128,340,1,"F"],["t",124,289,"O","m"],["l",122.2,314,122.2,294,""],["l",125.8,314,125.8,294,""],["l",111.6,319.4,81,303.2,""],["t",73,303,"F","m"],["l",135.3,317.8,163.7,297.3,""],["t",171,296,"F","m"],["l",112,333.3,80.7,352.3,""],["t",73,361,"F","m"],["l",135.7,333.7,163.5,352,""],["t",171,361,"F","m"],["t",195,332,"Square pyramidal",""],["t",13,407,"XeO\u2083",""],["t",109,399,"Xe","m"],["c",105,382,1,"F"],["c",113,382,1,"F"],["t",73,424,"O","m"],["l",98.5,404.5,80.6,416.9,""],["l",96.5,401.5,78.5,414,""],["t",149,424,"O","m"],["l",121.8,400.9,143.2,414.2,""],["l",119.9,403.9,141.3,417.3,""],["t",110,442,"O","m"],["l",111.1,407,111.6,429,""],["l",107.5,407,108,429,""],["t",181,402,"Pyramidal",""]]}},{id:"neet-2018-q062",pn:62,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Halogens",q:"Which of the following statements is not true for halogens ?",o:["All form monobasic oxyacids","All are oxidizing agents","All but fluorine show positive oxidation states","Chlorine has the highest electron-gain enthalpy"],a:"Bonus",s:`Fluorine shows only \u20131 oxidation state, so (3) is true.
Chlorine has the most negative electron-gain enthalpy, so (4) is true.
All halogens are oxidising agents, so (2) is true.
Fluorine forms only HOF as an oxyacid, which makes (1) doubtful.
Note: Printed answer is Bonus (no single correct option kept).`},{id:"neet-2018-q063",pn:63,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Group 13 hexafluoro ions",q:"Which one of the following elements is unable to form MF\u2086\xB3\u207B ion ?",o:["Ga","Al","B","In"],c:2,s:`MF\u2086\xB3\u207B needs the central atom to use d orbitals (sp\xB3d\xB2 hybridisation).
Boron is in period 2 and has no d orbitals, so it cannot form BF\u2086\xB3\u207B.
Al, Ga and In have vacant d orbitals and can form it.`},{id:"neet-2018-q065",pn:65,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Ellingham diagram",q:"Considering Ellingham diagram, which of the following metals can be used to reduce alumina ?",o:["Fe","Zn","Mg","Cu"],c:2,s:`On an Ellingham diagram a metal can reduce the oxide of another metal if its own oxide line lies below that of the oxide.
The Mg \u2192 MgO line is below the Al \u2192 Al\u2082O\u2083 line.
Fe, Zn and Cu lie above, so only Mg can reduce alumina.`},{id:"neet-2018-q066",pn:66,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Atomic radii of group 13",q:"The correct order of atomic radii in group 13 elements is",o:["B < Al < In < Ga < Tl","B < Al < Ga < In < Tl","B < Ga < Al < Tl < In","B < Ga < Al < In < Tl"],c:3,s:`Radius normally increases down the group.
Ga is slightly smaller than Al because the 3d electrons shield poorly, so Zeff is higher.
Order: B < Ga < Al < In < Tl.`}]})();
